;;; `std.contradiction/clash-0` — a fact of no argument beside its negation:
;;; the rule concludes `(false)`.
;;;
;;; ── What this file and its six siblings say ────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A state that holds a fact and its stored negation is dead only by a rule
;;; that says so: the kernel has no check of its own (Q-M1f.557).
;;; `clash-0` to `clash-6` are that rule, one a count of arguments, each
;;; concluding `(false)` with the pair as its premises, and the rule every
;;; program gets ends the state that holds it.
;;;
;;; So the claim below, `(false)`, *every node is dead*, holds of this program
;;; by the rule: without it, root would be alive and hold both facts. The
;;; firing itself, its premises the pair and its conclusion `(false)`, a fact
;;; root then holds, is asserted in-process, in
;;; `ein-infer/tests/contradiction_rules.rs`.
;;;
;;; The file where a claim sees the rules silent is `08_clash_satisfied.ein`:
;;; all seven loaded, activated, over facts and denials that do not meet. A
;;; rule that fired there would turn a model into a contradiction.
;;;
;;; ── One rule a file ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The import alone activates the rules for what the program declares
;;; (`clash-of-0` to `clash-of-6`), so each firing file declares one relation,
;;; and only the rule of its count can fire: here `clash-0`, for `raining`.
;;; The activator is written as well, which is how a program asks for the rule
;;; for a relation it does not declare.

(import std.contradiction :symbols (clash-0))

(relation raining :why "it is raining")

(clash-0 raining)

(raining)
(not (raining))          ; ⊥ — the fact and its denial

;; Root alone (Q-M1f.254): the question is root's verdict.
(solve
  :no-search
  :expect (false))

(query
  :goal (raining))
